<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molly Malone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first printed <a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Molly Malone&quot;, also known as &quot;Cockles and Mussels&quot; or &quot;In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>'s Fair City&quot;, is a song set in Dublin that has become the city's unofficial anthem. It tells the fictional tale of a fishwife who sold her wares in the streets of Dublin and died young, of a fever.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late 20th century a legend grew up that a historical Molly Malone lived in the 17th century, usually shown as a hawker by day and a part-time prostitute by night, though she has also been portrayed as one of the few chaste female street hawkers of her day. There is no evidence that the song is based on a real woman of that or any other time. Molly was a familiar form of Mary and Margaret, and many Molly Malones were born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> over the centuries, but nothing connects any of them to the song. In 1988 the Dublin Millennium Commission nevertheless endorsed claims made for a Mary Malone who died on 13 June 1699, and proclaimed 13 June Molly Malone Day.</p><p>The song is not recorded earlier than 1876, when it was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, Massachusetts, in a section of a book titled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> from English and German Universities, which suggests an Irish origin. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> edition of 1884 credited it to James Yorkston of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and said it was reprinted from an edition that implies a first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, but no copy has been found. Siobhán Marie Kilfeather places it in the music hall style of its period and calls the story of a historical Molly nonsense, while not wholly excluding that it rests on an older folk song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Several earlier pieces share elements with it. A song called Widow Malone was published as early as 1809, Pat Corney's Account of Himself as early as 1826, and Meet Me Miss Molly Malone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> in 1836. A copy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>'s Medley of about 1790, found again in 2010, holds a song to Sweet Molly Malone of Howth that otherwise resembles it not at all. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>'s fair city was a common phrase for the city, and alive, alive O was a cry of street vendors selling oysters, mussels, fish and eels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>A statue of Molly Malone by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> sculptor Jeanne Rynhart was unveiled on Grafton Street in 1988 by the Lord Mayor, Ben Briscoe, during the Dublin Millennium celebrations. Some artists objected to it, and it was nicknamed The Tart with the Cart. In 2014 it was moved to Suffolk Street to make way for Luas tracks. The Dubliners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, Danny Kaye, U2 and Sinéad O'Connor are among those who recorded the song, and the Irish soap opera Fair City takes its title from the first line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molly Malone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
